# The ferry town's café by the harbour: ordering, anything else, and the bill.
lesson language-at-the-cafe v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=4 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "At the café"
  goal "Order food and drink politely at a café in [[language.name]], and ask for the bill."

  look {
    scene 37x26 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        sign menu lines=["[[cafe.juice]] 2 €", "[[cafe.sandwich]] 5 €", "[[cafe.ice-cream]] 4 €", "[[cafe.water]] 1 €"] kind=board color=mint
        comic order who=["gran", "girl", "gran", "girl"] says=["[[cafe.what-would-you-like]]", "[[cafe.i-would-like-an-orange-juice]]", "[[cafe.anything-else]]", "[[cafe.nothing-else-thank-you]]"] moods=["happy", "happy", "happy", "happy"] with=["", "", "", ""] thinks=[0, 0, 0, 0] holds=["", "", "", ""] labels=["1", "2", "3", "4"]
      }
    }
    say """
      At the café by the harbour the waiter asks [[cafe.what-would-you-like]], what would you like?
      Ana answers [[cafe.i-would-like-an-orange-juice]], I would like an orange juice. The waiter
      asks [[cafe.anything-else]], anything else? [[cafe.nothing-else-thank-you]], nothing else,
      thank you. At the end: [[cafe.the-bill-please]], the bill, please.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.at-the-cafe-name k=4
    }
    show language.at-the-cafe-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.at-the-cafe-meaning k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show language.at-the-cafe-write k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.at-the-cafe-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=6
    show language.at-the-cafe-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.at-the-cafe-write k=4 easy-k=2 hard-k=6
    show language.at-the-cafe-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.at-the-cafe-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.at-the-cafe-write k=4
    }
  }
  language es {
    try stars=2 {
      show language.es-at-the-cafe-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    }
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.at-the-cafe-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[cafe.what-would-you-like]] [[cafe.i-would-like-a-juice-please]] [[cafe.anything-else]] [[cafe.nothing-else-thank-you]] [[cafe.the-bill-please]]"
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is the right phrase at the wrong moment: asking for the bill before
    ordering, or answering anything else with an order. The naming questions each describe a moment,
    and the near miss is the phrase for the moment next to it. The two-star question is about
    politeness, which each language marks its own way. From this grade the written words are marked
    with their accents: a word typed without its accent is not taken. The dictation is read from
    this sheet, where it prints as Read aloud, or played by the device. If the child is stuck, play
    café at home with a menu card, taking turns as the waiter. Off the page, order in the language
    at a café or at the table. It meets England's key stage 2 languages programme (years 3 to 6,
    statutory from age 7): "engage in conversations; ask and answer questions; express opinions and
    respond to those of others; seek clarification and help"; Japan's grades 5 and 6 foreign
    languages: "Can give instructions, make requests and respond to them by using basic
    expressions"; and CEFR A2, "Can communicate in simple and routine tasks requiring a simple and
    direct exchange of information on familiar and routine matters". The phrases are in
    [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[cafe.how-much-is-it]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[cafe.what-would-you-like]] and [[cafe.i-would-like-an-orange-juice]] and the words to write are [[cafe.sandwich]] and [[cafe.ice-cream]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[cafe.for-me-a-sandwich]] and [[cafe.nothing-else-thank-you]] and the words to write are [[cafe.i-would-like]], [[cafe.something]] and [[cafe.water]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
